\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\title{Least Squares --- Lecture Notes}
\author{Econometrics II}
\date{Week 5}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Setup}
We observe pairs $(y_i, x_i)$ for $i = 1, \dots, n$, with $y_i$ a
scalar outcome and $x_i$ a $k$-vector of regressors that includes a
constant. The linear model relates the two through an unknown
parameter vector,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:model}
y_i = x_i^{\top} \beta + u_i
\end{equation}
where $u_i$ collects everything the regressors leave unexplained.
Stacking the observations gives the matrix form $y = X\beta + u$, with
$X$ of dimension $n \times k$ and full column rank.

\section{Estimation}
Minimising the sum of squared residuals over $\beta$ leads to the
normal equations
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:normal}
X^{\top} X \, \hat{\beta} = X^{\top} y
\end{equation}
which have a unique solution because $X^{\top} X$ is invertible under
the rank condition. Identification of the projection coefficient rests
on exogeneity,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:exog}
E[u_i \mid x_i] = 0
\end{equation}
and consistency follows from the law of large numbers applied to the
sample moments,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:lln}
\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i u_i \xrightarrow{p} 0
\end{equation}
Solving the normal equations for $\hat{\beta}$ then gives the
closed-form estimator
\begin{equation*}
\hat{\beta} = (X^\top X)^{-1} X^\top y
\end{equation*}
As shown in (5), the OLS estimator is consistent under the exogeneity
assumption, provided the second moments of the regressors exist.

\section{Inference}
Under homoskedasticity the usual variance estimator is the residual
sum of squares divided by $n - k$, times the inverse cross-product
matrix. Heteroskedasticity-robust alternatives replace the middle
matrix with a weighted sum of outer products and are recommended by
default in applied work.

\section{Common pitfalls}
Dropping the constant from $x_i$ silently forces the regression
through the origin, which rarely makes sense in economic applications.
Perfect collinearity is caught by the rank condition, but near
collinearity is not: it inflates variances without triggering any
error, and the only symptom is instability across nearby samples.

\section{Exercises}
Derive the normal equations from first principles. Show that the
residuals are orthogonal to the column space of $X$. Verify that the
estimator is unbiased conditional on $X$ under exogeneity, and
construct a counterexample when exogeneity fails.

\end{document}

